#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "105a5a4a-2fea-54bb-97c6-c2a700b05f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5548" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - t^2$." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖ = \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{p}(t) = 1-t^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad p(2)= -3,\\qquad p(3)=-8\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖p(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{p(1)p(1) + p(2)p(2) + p(3)p(3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2} + [p(2)]^{2} + [p(3)]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (-8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 9 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{73}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5aafdd7d-939a-5690-ac4d-46b554524d75" :question/math-academy-id "e-5549" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1$." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(2t - 1)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(4t^{2} - 4t + 1)dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4t^{3}}{3} - 2t^{2} + t) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 2 + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d279bb1d-754e-5044-9a01-d4c86f30b269" :question/math-academy-id "e-5550" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t$." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{π}(3t)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}9t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 3π^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\| = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t)$, we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= (f(t))/(‖f(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π\\sqrt{3π}} \\cdot 3t \\\\\n&= \\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{π\\sqrt{π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "35e7ce1a-b2cc-50dd-8bf7-24d20e55d623" :question/math-academy-id "e-5908" :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖v‖ = \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖v‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3) + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (1 \\cdot 1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18 + 4 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb729d0-97da-52b8-9d80-164e9e46a853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132393} {:answer/id #uuid "18a4a8c1-2d56-51db-a64c-2686de79d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132394} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad725d4-4e91-5b33-9afe-1b3839e539ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132395} {:answer/id #uuid "38e27199-ce70-587d-afbc-4d70e86e05dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132396} {:answer/id #uuid "4837fdcc-4024-5a9d-b1e5-a9caf935c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad725d4-4e91-5b33-9afe-1b3839e539ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.typ